{"item": "T-0053", "job": "adjudicate-t-0053", "kind": "ruling", "product": "asf", "text": "What was disputed was never the code — it was whether T-0053's work existed at all. The item was held three times as \"unpushed\", but `git ls-remote origin worker/T-0053` returns b858b97, the same sha as HEAD: the commit has been on origin since the first session. The hold was a phantom, produced by the branch's upstream being configured as `origin/main` (`branch.worker/T-0053.merge = refs/heads/main`), which makes `git status` print \"Your branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit. (use git push to publish your local commits)\" — indistinguishable from genuinely unpushed work, three rounds running. The hold is therefore overruled, and being overruled it never returns in a later round. On the merits the work stands as committed: I checked each disputed anchor against the checkout myself — D10's row identity holds the Task and not the Feature (asf/feeder/rows.py:330-340, matching docs/plans/f-0086.md:119), P7's KIND_ORDER[RESHAPE] = 3 with the bumped fallback KIND_ORDER.get(r.kind, 4) is in place (asf/feeder/rows.py:365,425), both hold rows are non-launching because Row.launches tests action.startswith('would launch') and neither joins `running`, and all five tests the plan names are present and green, with the full 971-test suite and check_generic clean. Nothing is upheld and no edit was made; what changes is the tracking ref, which I repointed to origin/worker/T-0053 so the lane reports its own state, and T-0053 is ready to close on the commit it has had all along.\nNEEDS OPERATOR: the hold detector reads \"ahead of <upstream>\" rather than comparing the lane against its own remote ref, so any worker branch cut with upstream main will be held as unpushed forever — recommend it compare `git rev-parse HEAD` to `git ls-remote origin <branch>`; needs a bug card minted by `asf new` (I must not invent the id).", "ts": "2026-09-22T18:52:38Z"}
{"item": "T-0036", "job": "adjudicate-t-0036", "kind": "ruling", "product": "asf", "text": "What was disputed was never a review finding — no `docs/reviews/1-t-0036.md` exists on the branch, on `origin/main`, or anywhere in history (`docs/reviews/` holds only `1-b-0005-ruling.md`), and no round-1 review was ever written. The hold's own reason is `conflict`, and the conflict is literal: the worktree sat mid-rebase with `asf/conventions.py` unmerged in three hunks, and it came back three times because both sides are correct and additive, so every \"correction\" round re-derived the same standoff instead of resolving it. HEAD carries the harvest block (`DEFAULT_HARVEST_GATE`, `DEFAULT_BRANCHES_PER_TICK`, `DEFAULT_GATE_TIMEOUT_S`, `HARVEST_KEYS` and the three dataclass fields, from B-0040/B-0031/B-0072); `a0efb42` carries T-0036's nine `DEFAULT_*` constants and nine fields. Neither supersedes the other. What now holds: both sides survive in full — in the docstring example `briefs_dir: docs/briefs` precedes the `harvest:` block (a top-level key before a nested one, so the yaml stays readable); in the constants the harvest group keeps `HARVEST_KEYS` adjacent to its own three, with the nine `None` defaults following it; in the dataclass the three harvest fields precede the nine, all before `stage_limits`/`extra`. I checked the count that the two sides could have broken between them: `forbidden_patterns()` still returns exactly nine patterns, because the nine new defaults are `None` (not `str`) and `DEFAULT_HARVEST_GATE = 'combined'` holds no `/`, `{` or `#` — `python3 -m asf.conventions --forbidden` prints the nine, and `ForbiddenPatternsTests.test_one_pattern_per_path_shaped_default` asserts it. Two findings I overrule, and neither returns in a later round: first, the card names `tests.test_conventions.CheckConventionsScriptTests.test_a_copied_default_fails_the_build`, which has never existed under that name in any branch (`git log -S --all` over the file returns nothing) — the class exists and its four tests, including `test_the_whole_package_is_clean`, cover the behaviour and pass, so the card's test name is a naming slip, not a missing test; second, `docs/plans/f-0074.md` contains the string `T-0036` zero times — it names its work \"Task 1\" at line 66, which is this card, so the plan is not silent on it and no plan edit is owed. What changes: the rebase is finished, the tree is clean, the single signed-off commit `9729a62` sits one ahead of `origin/main` with the full suite green, and `asf/conventions.py:6` still reads \"the second and last exception\" untouched, as PD8 reserves that wording for Task 7. The push was refused as non-fast-forward, which is the rebase and nothing else; per B-0056 I did not merge, force or recut — the factory publishes the rebased lane branch. The loop ends here.", "ts": "2026-09-22T18:52:38Z"}
{"item": "T-0041", "job": "adjudicate-t-0041", "kind": "ruling", "product": "asf", "text": "The stalemate was naming, and both sides were partly right, so the file decided each anchor separately. UPHELD, and fixed: the cache assertion named cache-backlog-evidence.json, but _cache_file joins \"cache-\" to a base of \"evidence.json\" (asf/evidence/evidence.py:70,1018), so the path is cache-evidence.json — the name the plan's step 6 and the spec's line 452 already carried; the gate was genuinely red on this and is now green. UPHELD as to the name only: test_the_spec_brief_cites_the_samples_dirs drove --kind review, which contradicted its own name; the case is renamed test_the_review_brief_cites_the_samples_dirs. UPHELD and added: the cache case now owns a TMPDIR and asserts nothing was written there, and the evidence case asserts superpowers, sdd-input and feature-matrix are absent — both were the spec's stated acceptance and had been dropped. OVERRULED, and these never come back in a later round: the plan's asf evidence --json is stale — asf/cli.py:100 gives p_evidence only --fresh; the plan's features['f-0002']['spec_doc'][0] is stale — asf/evidence/evidence.py:610 serialises the key as \"spec\" holding a plain string, which is what the coder wrote and what passes; and the coder's substitution of --kind review for the plan's bare brief invocation stands, because asf/briefs/preamble.py:348 emits the review path only if kind in REVIEW_KINDS, so no spec-kind body can satisfy step 4's two assertions at once — the plan's step 4 is wrong on this point, not the branch. OVERRULED as a finding against this session, but recorded as debt: the fourth case, test_init_creates_the_intake_dir_not_inbox, cannot be written green today on two independent counts I verified — nothing reads conventions.intake_dir (the field is at asf/conventions.py:100 and appears nowhere under asf/groom, asf/record or asf/tick, so Task 5 is unlanded), and this fixture's asf init takes the adopt branch at asf/init.py:275, so lay_down, the only caller that creates the stream folders, never runs here, as test_init_adopts_the_ten_cards proves. Writing it red would fail the gate and dropping it silently would lose F-0074's PD5 proof, so it lands skipped with both blockers named in the skip reason. What changes: T-0041's branch is gate-green and lands as it stands; Task 5 of F-0074 inherits the skipped case and must un-skip it, and whoever revisits the plan should correct step 4's bare brief invocation, step 5's --json and spec_doc[0], since the code has moved past all three.", "ts": "2026-09-22T18:52:38Z"}
